# ⚠ `supabase/functions/deno.lock` USED TO BE IGNORED HERE, and it is now COMMITTED.
# The rule that stood until 2026-09-02 read: "Deliberately NOT committed: CI has
# never validated against one, and adding it introduces a lock-mismatch failure
# mode on remote-dep drift for a gate that is now blocking."
#
# That is a cost stated with no number in it, and the other side of the trade now
# has one. Without a lock the edge-deno job RE-RESOLVES the whole graph on every
# run, so it is wall-clock dependent: Deno refuses any version published inside
# its minimum-dependency-age window (default 24 h), and it does not backtrack to
# an older parent when a required sub-dependency is still too young. supabase-js
# 2.113.0 aged in at 2026-09-03T06:04:34Z and its own auth-js 2.113.0 at
# 06:05:01Z, so for those 27 seconds there was NO resolvable graph — measured, one
# hard failure at 06:04:15Z in the last 60 runs, self-healing, and it recurs on
# every Supabase release whose sub-packages publish seconds apart.
#
# The claimed cost does not materialise: `deno check` is NOT frozen, so it
# rewrites a drifted lock rather than failing on it. Measured with Deno 2.9.6
# against a control the repo can re-run: a floating `^3` range locked to a
# version only 11 h old still resolves and passes under the DEFAULT 24 h policy,
# while the same version with no lock is a hard error. See
# `__tests__/edge-function-deps-are-locked.test.ts`.

# `git format-patch` hand-offs from Cowork sessions that cannot push land here as
# an untracked directory at the repo ROOT (e.g. "Rip Packs City/cowork-2026-08-25/").
# Once the patches are applied their content lives in git history, so the directory
# is pure redundancy — but until someone deletes it, a `git add -A` would commit the
# patch files themselves. Ignore it rather than rely on everyone using explicit paths.
Rip Packs City/

# Cowork desktop-VM push credential (2026-08-29): never commit, never print
.rpc-git-cred

# 🚨 NOTHING UNDER docs/ MAY BE SILENTLY IGNORED — added 2026-09-03 after an incident.
#
# A parking commit wrote docs/overnight/handoffs/<name>.patch, ran `git add -A`, saw a
# clean `git status`, and pushed. `*.patch` above swallowed the file SILENTLY: the commit
# carried only the README, whose "Live" entry then pointed at nothing and whose ledger
# entry said "the patch is committed". The next session found a promise, not an artifact.
#
# ⚠ `git add` does not report what an ignore rule skipped, and a clean `git status`
# afterwards is CONSISTENT with the file having been added. The two observations a writer
# naturally makes cannot tell the two outcomes apart — which is why this is a rule and not
# a note telling people to look harder.
#
# ⭐ AND `*.patch` WAS NOT THE ONLY TRAP. Measured the same day, every one of these would
# have been swallowed under docs/: `logs/` (so docs/logs/ entirely), `imports/`,
# `sweep-*.log`, `*.pem`, `*creds*.json`. Six of six are addable after this rule.
#
# docs/ exists to be READ FROM THE REPO. A file there that only the authoring sandbox can
# see is worse than absent, because it reads as present to its author.
# ⚠ MUST STAY ABOVE THE SECRET RE-ASSERTION BELOW, and below everything it overrides:
# a negation only overrides patterns that come BEFORE it.
!docs/**

# ⛔ …BUT NEVER SECRETS, EVEN UNDER docs/. Re-asserted AFTER the negation, because a blanket
# `!docs/**` would otherwise make an `.env.local`, a `*creds*.json` or a `*.pem` dropped in
# docs/ committable. This repo has already paid for a credential leak once (the 2026-08-03
# filter-repo purge), so the convenience rule above must not quietly widen that surface.
# ⚠ These duplicate patterns above ON PURPOSE — a duplicate is the mechanism, not an
# oversight, and deleting one as redundant re-opens the hole under docs/.
.env*.local
*creds*.json
*-creds.json
*.pem
